Three CoreFoundation functions that were declared and never defined, and an error provider.

CFStringTokenizerCopyBestStringLanguage was COMMENTED OUT with a note that it needs a language
model. It answers from Unicode script instead, which is a different and smaller question, said so in
the code: a script that belongs to one language gives the right answer, Latin and Cyrillic get their
most common one, and a string with no script at all answers NULL as the documentation allows.

CFURLClearResourcePropertyCache and CFURLClearResourcePropertyCacheForKey were missing entirely,
although the cache they clear is real: every resource property a CFURL answers is kept in its own
resourceInfo dictionary, and CFURL.h says the client owns that lifetime.

NSMultipleUnderlyingErrorsKey sits next to NSUnderlyingErrorKey, where its singular form already is.

AND +[NSError setUserInfoValueProviderForDomain:provider:], which is not a name but a mechanism: a
domain registers one block that is asked for a userInfo key when someone wants it, instead of filling
a dictionary at the moment the error is made. It was an unrecognised selector, and iA Writer calls it
from a +load, so the application died before its first window with no message beyond the selector.
Registering is stored per domain under its own lock, and every accessor that reads a specific key now
asks the provider when the dictionary does not already carry the value.

